      "text": "Given the expression $\\left\\lceil\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}}\\right\\rceil+\\left\\lceil\\frac{9}{4}\\right\\rceil+\\left\\lceil\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)^2\\right\\rceil$, we can break it down step by step:\n\n1. Evaluate $\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}}$:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{9}}{\\sqrt{4}} = \\frac{3}{2} = 1.5\n\\]\nThe ceiling of $1.5$ is $2$:\n\\[\n\\left\\lceil\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}}\\right\\rceil = \\left\\lceil1.5\\right\\rceil = 2\n\\]\n\n2. Evaluate $\\frac{9}{4}$:\n\\[\n\\frac{9}{4} = 2.25\n\\]\nThe ceiling of $2.25$ is $3$:\n\\[\n\\left\\lceil\\frac{9}{4}\\right\\rceil = \\left\\lceil2.25\\right\\rceil = 3\n\\]\n\n3. Evaluate $\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)^2$:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)^2 = \\frac{81}{16} = 5.0625\n\\]\nThe ceiling of $5.0625$ is $6$:\n\\[\n\\left\\lceil\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)^2\\right\\rceil = \\left\\lceil5.0625\\right\\rceil = 6\n\\]\n\nNow, summing up these results:\n\\[\n\\left\\lceil\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}}\\right\\rceil+\\left\\lceil\\frac{9}{4}\\right\\rceil+\\left\\lceil\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)^2\\right\\rceil = 2 + 3 + 6 = 11\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is $\\boxed{11}$.",
